Developmental dysplasia of the hip (Perthes)
Disabilities & Congenital Disorders
Positive Barlow/Ortolani manoeuvres, Asymmetrical gluteal creases, Limited hip abduction, Galeazzi sign

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-016
Reason for Visit: Acute parental distress regarding infant leg posture and pain during nappy changes.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Infant, mother, and grandmother
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a 4-month-old infant with acute, severe crying specifically triggered by hip abduction during nappy changes over the past 6 hours, accompanied by a suddenly pronounced leg length discrepancy. Assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period due to the necessity of ruling out an acute septic joint versus an unstable, painfully dislocated hip requiring immediate orthopedic immobilization.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Her left leg looks suddenly shorter than the right, and she screams in agony if I try to open her legs to change her nappy.
History of Present Illness: The mother reports a 6-hour history of severe distress during routine handling. Historically, the infant had mild asymmetrical gluteal creases noted by the maternal grandmother but was otherwise well. Today, the mother noticed a sudden apparent limb length discrepancy (left leg appearing shorter). Any attempt to abduct the left hip causes the infant to cry inconsolably. There is no history of trauma, but there is a maternal family history of hip dysplasia. The infant has not been bearing weight (pre-ambulatory) but has stopped spontaneous kicking of the left leg over the past few hours.
Functional Status: Fully dependent infant; spontaneous movement of the left lower limb is currently reduced.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme maternal anxiety; risk of delayed orthopedic intervention causing permanent joint damage.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 85/50 mmHg | HR: 155 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 35 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 6.2 Kg
General: Infant is calm while swaddled but becomes highly distressed upon exposure and manipulation of the lower limbs.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal exam reveals a clear Galeazzi sign (left knee is lower than the right when hips and knees are flexed supine). There are deep, asymmetrical gluteal and thigh skin creases on the left. Examination of the left hip demonstrates severely limited active and passive hip abduction, guarded by muscle spasm and crying. A positive Barlow manoeuvre is elicited on the left, demonstrating posterior subluxation of the femoral head. A positive Ortolani manoeuvre yields a palpable 'clunk' as the dislocated hip is reduced, momentarily relieving the infant's distress. The right hip is stable. No erythema or warmth over the joint.
Environmental Observations: A narrow-based baby carrier is present in the living room, which may exacerbate hip adduction and dislocation.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications; paracetamol given 2 hours ago with minimal effect on the movement-related pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Developmental dysplasia of the hip (Perthes) (with acute painful subluxation/dislocation) - Status: Acute complication
Differentials:

Septic Arthritis of the Hip - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of fever, absence of joint erythema/warmth, and the mechanical, reproducible nature of the clunk (Ortolani) which implies instability rather than isolated infection.

Femoral Fracture - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of trauma, lack of localized bony swelling or crepitus, and the specific physical signs pointing directly to the joint capsule.

Transient Synovitis - Ruled out/unlikely given the age of the patient (more common in toddlers), the lack of a preceding viral illness, and the presence of objective mechanical instability (positive Barlow/Ortolani).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The presentation of acute pain with limited abduction and an apparent shortened limb in an infant required urgent physical evaluation. The objective findings of asymmetrical creases, a positive Galeazzi sign, and palpable mechanical instability (Barlow/Ortolani clunk) confirmed an acutely subluxated/dislocated dysplastic hip. This assessment could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period because the acute muscle spasm and pain indicated an unstable joint, and the parents required immediate instruction on safe handling and positioning to prevent further cartilage damage and extreme distress prior to definitive orthopedic management.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed the parents on strict avoidance of hip adduction and extension. Demonstrated how to carry the infant with hips splayed (frog-leg position). Administered oral Ibuprofen for pain relief related to the joint capsule spasm.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you carry the baby straddled on your hip rather than in the narrow carrier, gravity naturally holds the joint in place; therefore, permanently switching to a wide-based carrying method directly treats her condition. When you roll her gently side-to-side for nappy changes instead of pulling her legs straight up, you avoid triggering the pain; practicing this new nappy routine tonight will ensure she sleeps comfortably.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral generated for a dynamic hip ultrasound and pediatric orthopedic review.
DME/Safety (Optional): Advised to immediately cease using the narrow-based baby carrier.
Follow-up: The family must attend the urgent pediatric orthopedic clinic tomorrow morning for a Pavlik harness fitting.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Paediatric Emergency Department or call 000 if the infant develops a fever above 38.0 C, if the leg becomes completely pale and cold, or if she refuses to feed completely due to pain.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The clinical diagnosis requires confirmation via ultrasound; the hip is currently unstable and prone to slipping out of the socket.
Clinical Course: With appropriate bracing (e.g., Pavlik harness), the joint capsule will tighten and the bone will remodel normally in the vast majority of cases.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek urgent medical review if you notice any sudden swelling or redness over the hip, or if the pain cannot be managed with paracetamol and ibuprofen tonight.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The hip socket is too shallow, allowing the ball of the thigh bone to slide out, which stretches the joint capsule and causes intense pain. Keeping her legs wide open naturally pushes the ball back into the center of the socket.
Actionable Steps: Do not pull the legs straight. Carry her straddled on your hip. Attend the orthopedic appointment tomorrow morning.

